---
import Layout from "../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Breadcrumbs from "../../../components/layout/Breadcrumbs.astro";
import { COMPANY } from "../../../data/company";
---

<Layout
  title="7 Smart Tips to Get a Non-QM Mortgage When You're Self-Employed | Cliffco Mortgage Bankers"
  description="Practical advice for self-employed borrowers navigating the non-QM mortgage process with bank statements, 1099s, P&L docs, and more."
  type="article"
>
  <Breadcrumbs trail={[
    { name: "Home", href: "/" },
    { name: "Learn", href: "/learn/" },
    { name: "Non-QM Self-Employed Guide", href: "/learn/non-qm-mortgage-self-employed/" },
  ]} />

  <section class="container-wide pt-12 pb-12">
    <p class="eyebrow">Self-Employed</p>
    <h1 class="mt-3" style="font-size: var(--text-display-lg);">7 Smart Tips to Get a Non-QM Mortgage When You're Self-Employed</h1>
    <p class="lead mt-6">
      Being self-employed doesn't disqualify you from buying a home or growing a real estate portfolio.
      It just means you need a lender who knows how to read your financial picture accurately.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="bg-white pb-12">
    <div class="container-prose">

      <p>
        The conventional mortgage system was built around W-2 income. Two years of tax returns, a few
        pay stubs, done. For the self-employed, the picture is messier and far less accurately captured
        by tax returns that were optimized for deductions, not loan applications.
      </p>
      <p>
        Non-QM mortgages close that gap. Using bank statements, 1099s, P&amp;L statements, or DSCR on
        investment properties, self-employed borrowers can qualify based on actual cash flow rather than
        taxable income. Here are seven practical tips to help you navigate the process.
      </p>

      <h2>What You'll Generally Need to Get Started</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>1 to 2 years of self-employment history</li>
        <li>12 to 24 months of personal or business bank statements</li>
        <li>1099 forms, P&amp;L statements, or CPA-prepared income documentation</li>
        <li>At least 10% down payment (some programs go to 90% LTV)</li>
        <li>A business license or other evidence of active self-employment, if applicable</li>
        <li>Consistent cash flow, demonstrated through statements or documentation</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Tip 1: Know Your Alternative Income Documentation Options</h2>
      <p>
        One of the biggest advantages of non-QM is the variety of ways income can be documented. You
        don't have to pick just one. Common options include:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Bank statements (12 to 24 months):</strong> The most commonly used method. Lenders analyze your average monthly deposits to establish an income figure. Particularly powerful for borrowers whose tax returns show lower income due to business deductions.</li>
        <li><strong>1099 income:</strong> Works well for independent contractors and frequent 1099 earners.</li>
        <li><strong>Profit and Loss statements:</strong> A CPA-prepared P&amp;L gives the lender a structured view of business income and expenses. Some programs accept P&amp;L without supporting bank statements.</li>
        <li><strong>DSCR documentation:</strong> If you're buying investment property, rental income from leases or market analysis can qualify the loan entirely, bypassing your personal income.</li>
        <li><strong>Asset depletion:</strong> Investment accounts, retirement accounts, and savings can be converted to a qualifying monthly income figure for borrowers with significant assets.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Tip 2: Keep Your Finances Organized and Separated</h2>
      <p>
        This is practical advice that pays off specifically at mortgage time. A few habits that make
        your file stronger:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Keep business and personal bank accounts completely separate. Mixed accounts create ambiguity about what counts as income and what is a business expense pass-through.</li>
        <li>Take regular, documented owner's draws or salary distributions rather than sporadic large transfers.</li>
        <li>Maintain consistent deposit patterns. Large, irregular deposits followed by silence are harder to underwrite than steady monthly activity.</li>
        <li>Clean documentation speeds up the process significantly. Disorganized bank statements with excessive transfers between accounts add time and questions.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Tip 3: Work With a Loan Officer Who Specializes in Non-QM</h2>
      <p>
        This matters more than people realize. A loan officer who runs non-QM files regularly knows
        which program fits your income type, which lenders offer the best pricing for your credit profile,
        and how to present your file in the strongest possible light. A generalist who does one
        non-QM loan per quarter will take longer, make more mistakes, and likely miss options that
        could save you money.
      </p>
      <p>
        At Cliffco, non-QM is one of our four core products. Our team originates these loans across
        32 states and can identify quickly whether bank statement, P&amp;L, 1099, or DSCR is the
        right structure for your situation.
      </p>

      <h2>Tip 4: Understand How Your Income Will Actually Be Evaluated</h2>
      <p>
        Different lenders use different methods. Here's what most are looking at for a bank statement
        loan:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Average monthly gross deposits over 12 or 24 months (with business expense ratios applied for business accounts)</li>
        <li>P&amp;L trends: is income growing, stable, or declining?</li>
        <li>Credit score and utilization</li>
        <li>Debt-to-income ratio based on the qualifying income figure</li>
        <li>Cash reserves after the down payment</li>
        <li>For investment property: DSCR ratio as a supplement or replacement for personal income</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Knowing how the math works lets you make decisions in advance. If you're running a lot of
        transfers between accounts that inflate the raw deposit total, a good non-QM lender will
        help you strip those out to find a clean income number.
      </p>

      <h2>Tip 5: Get Your Credit Profile in Good Shape First</h2>
      <p>
        Non-QM programs accept credit scores starting around 620, but a better score means better rates.
        Before you apply:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Pull your credit reports and dispute any errors. Incorrect late payments or accounts that aren't yours can drag your score down unnecessarily.</li>
        <li>Keep utilization below 30% on all revolving accounts. Below 10% is even better.</li>
        <li>Pay down card balances rather than shifting them around.</li>
        <li>Avoid applying for new credit in the months before your mortgage application. New inquiries and new accounts lower your score temporarily.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Credit matters more, not less, when your income documentation is non-traditional. A strong
        credit profile signals reliability and gives the lender more confidence in the file.
      </p>

      <h2>Tip 6: Be Ready to Explain Income Irregularities</h2>
      <p>
        Self-employed income is rarely perfectly smooth. Seasonal slowdowns, a quarter where you invested
        heavily in equipment, a gap between large contracts — these things happen and lenders see them
        all the time. What matters is that you can explain them clearly.
      </p>
      <p>
        A brief letter of explanation from you (and sometimes a note from your CPA) that puts context
        around a dip in deposits or a one-time large withdrawal goes a long way. Lenders aren't looking
        for perfection. They're looking for patterns they can rely on and a borrower who is transparent
        about what happened.
      </p>

      <h2>Tip 7: Choose the Right Loan Product for Your Specific Situation</h2>
      <p>
        Not all non-QM programs are alike, and the best fit depends on how you earn and how you're
        structured:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Bank statement loan:</strong> Best for sole proprietors and business owners with high deposit volume and clear account activity.</li>
        <li><strong>DSCR:</strong> Best for real estate investors who want qualification based entirely on property cash flow.</li>
        <li><strong>P&amp;L only:</strong> Best for structured businesses with a reliable CPA and well-documented financials.</li>
        <li><strong>Asset depletion:</strong> Best for retirees or borrowers with significant investment or retirement accounts who have low reportable income.</li>
        <li><strong>1099 loan:</strong> Best for regular 1099 contractors who don't have a formal business structure but have consistent earnings history.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Common Questions From Self-Employed Borrowers</h2>

      <h3>I've only been self-employed for one year. Can I still qualify?</h3>
      <p>
        Some programs will consider borrowers with as little as one year of self-employment history,
        particularly if you have a prior employment history in the same field. Discuss your specific
        timeline with a loan officer early.
      </p>

      <h3>Do I need to provide tax returns?</h3>
      <p>
        For most non-QM programs, no. That's part of the appeal. Bank statements, 1099s, or P&amp;L
        statements can replace tax returns entirely. Some programs use tax returns as a supplement but
        don't require them for primary qualification.
      </p>

      <h3>Is it harder to get a mortgage when self-employed?</h3>
      <p>
        It's different, not harder, when you work with the right lender. The documentation requirements
        are different and the program options are different. With a non-QM specialist, the process is
        straightforward.
      </p>

      <h3>What credit score do I need?</h3>
      <p>Most non-QM programs start at 620. Better pricing is available at 680 and above.</p>

      <h3>How much can I borrow?</h3>
      <p>
        Some non-QM programs go up to 90% LTV, which means as little as 10% down. The exact limit
        depends on the program, your credit score, and the property type.
      </p>

      <h3>Can my DTI be higher than 43%?</h3>
      <p>Yes. Many non-QM programs allow debt-to-income ratios up to 50%.</p>

      <h3>How long does it take to close?</h3>
      <p>Most non-QM loans close in 30 to 45 days, which is comparable to conventional timelines.</p>

      <h3>Can I refinance an existing mortgage with a non-QM loan?</h3>
      <p>
        Yes. Rate-and-term refinances and cash-out refinances are both available through non-QM programs.
        This is a common move for self-employed borrowers who initially bought with a conventional loan
        but have seen their income reporting change since then.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="accent-gradient">
    <div class="container-wide py-16 text-center">
      <h2 class="text-white" style="font-size: var(--text-h2);">Ready to talk through your options?</h2>
      <p class="lead mt-4 text-white/85">A licensed Cliffco loan officer responds in one business day.</p>
      <div class="mt-8 flex justify-center gap-3 flex-wrap">
        <a href="/get-started/" class="btn btn-primary !bg-white !text-[var(--color-rich-black)]">Get a pre-qual</a>
        <a href={`tel:${COMPANY.hq.phoneE164}`} class="btn btn-secondary !border-white !text-white">{COMPANY.hq.phone}</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
